<html>
<head>
<meta name="KeyWords" content="Cormas, Cirad, gestion des ressources naturelles, natural resource management, INRM, environnement, environment, simulation, modelisation, modelling, SMA, MAS, multi-agent, smalltalk, visualworks, logiciel, software">
<title> Echanges economiques et organisations emergentes</title>
<meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=iso-8859-1">

<!--#include virtual="/include/script.inc" -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/cormas.css" -->
</head>
<body marginwidth="0" marginheight="0" topmargin="0" leftmargin="0" border="0" onLoad="MM_preloadImages(/images/en/accueil_r.gif,/images/en/demarche_r.gif,/images/en/logiciel_r.gif,/images/en/applications_r.gif,/images/en/biblio_r.gif,/images/en/formation_r.gif,/images/en/reseaux_r.gif)" bgcolor="#FFFFFF">
<!--#include virtual="haut.inc" -->
<table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" width="100%">
<tr>
  <!--#include virtual="sommair.inc" --> 
  <td width="*" valign="top" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"> 
 <p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>THIS PAGE WILL BE SOON AVAILABLE IN ENGLISH</p> 
   <p>&nbsp;
 
      <h1>Potlatch</h1>
      <h2>Echanges &eacute;conomiques et organisations &eacute;mergentes 
      </h2>
      <p>Juliette Rouchier, Manchester Metropolitan University.</p>
  
      <img src="/images/applica/jumel.jpg" width="245" height="169" align="right" vspace="10" hspace="10"> 
      <p>Juliette Rouchier travaille maintenant au Centre for Policy Modelling, 
        &agrave; la Manchester Metropolitan University. Le travail pr&eacute;sent&eacute; 
        ici correspond au travail de DEA.</p>
   <h2>Mod&eacute;liser les &eacute;changes de biens</h2>
      <p>Les trois mod&egrave;les d&eacute;velopp&eacute;s par Juliette Rouchier 
        sont ax&eacute;s principalement sur lanalyse de structures &eacute;mergentes 
        au sein du groupe, qui apparaissent grâce &agrave; la r&eacute;p&eacute;tition 
        dun grand nombre d&eacute;changes de biens.</p>
      <p>Le mod&egrave;le JuMel a &eacute;t&eacute; construit en sinspirant des 
        r&eacute;sultats du travail de terrain de M&eacute;lanie Requier-Desjardins. 
        Il est d&eacute;crit dans une autre <a href="jumel.htm">page</a>. Les 
        deux autres sont des travaux inspir&eacute;s de donn&eacute;es purement 
        th&eacute;oriques. Un des mod&egrave;les a &eacute;t&eacute; construit 
        avec Martin OConnor de lUniversit&eacute; de Saint Quentin en Yvelines. 
        Les interactions repr&eacute;sent&eacute;es prennent une forme inspir&eacute;e 
        des &eacute;changes de dons et contre-dons, cest lexemple du <b>potlatch</b>.</p>
      <p>Bient&ocirc;t vous en saurez plus.</p>
      <p> Vous pouvez <a href="mailto:j.rouchier@mmu.ac.uk">contacter lauteur</a>.</p>
   <!--#include virtual="/include/copyright_en.inc" -->
	</td>
  <td width="15" valign="top" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"> 
   <div align="right" class="texte"></div>
  </td>
 </tr>
</table>
</body>
</html>
<html>
<head>
<meta name="KeyWords" content="Cormas, Cirad, gestion des ressources naturelles, natural resource management, INRM, environnement, environment, simulation, modelisation, modelling, SMA, MAS, multi-agent, smalltalk, visualworks, logiciel, software">
<title>SimPolilla</title>
<meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=iso-8859-1">

<!--#include virtual="/include/script.inc" -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/cormas.css" -->
</head>
<body marginwidth="0" marginheight="0" topmargin="0" leftmargin="0" border="0" onLoad="MM_preloadImages(/images/fr/accueil_r.gif,/images/fr/demarche_r.gif,/images/fr/logiciel_r.gif,/images/fr/applications_r.gif,/images/fr/biblio_r.gif,/images/fr/formation_r.gif,/images/fr/reseaux_r.gif)" bgcolor="#FFFFFF">
<!--#include virtual="haut.inc" -->
<table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" width="100%">
<tr>
  <!--#include virtual="sommair.inc" --> 
    <td width="*" valign="top" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"> 
      <p>&nbsp; 
      <h1>SimPolilla&copy;<br>
        A cellular automaton to describe a pest invasion within the northern Andes</h1>
      <h1><font size="3">Fran&ccedil;ois Rebaudo<sup>1</sup>, V&eacute;ronica Crespo-P&eacute;rez<sup>1,2</sup>, 
        Olivier Dangles<sup>1,2</sup></font></h1>
      <p><sup>1</sup>IRD, UR 072, LEGS, UPR 9034, CNRS 91198 Gif-sur-Yvette Cedex 
        and Universit&eacute; Paris-Sud 11, 91405 Orsay Cedex, France<br>
        <sup>2</sup>Pontificia Universidad Cat&oacute;lica del Ecuador, Facultad 
        de Ciencias Naturales y Biol&oacute;gicas, Laboratorio de Entomolog&iacute;a, 
        Quito, Ecuador </p>
      <h3><img src="../../images/applica/SimPolilla.jpg" width="400" height="269" align="right">OVERVIEW</h3>
<h4>Purpose</h4>
<p>The SimPolilla model was developed to describe the invasion and diffusion of the potato tuber moths (PTM) (<i>Tecia solanivora</i>, <i>Phthorimaea operculella</i> and <i>Symmetrischema tangolias</i>, Gelechiidae, Lepidoptera), tiny moths that invaded the agricultural landscape of the North Andean region in the last decades. The larvae of these moths are serious pests of potatoes, one of the main food crops of the region. The model was developed and validated in a pilot region of central Ecuador but was built to be applicable to a much wider geographic range in North Andes.</p>
      <p>The model description follows the ODD protocol for describing individual- 
        and agent-based models (Polhill et al. 2008; Grimm et al. 2006; Grimm 
        and Railsback 2005) and cellular automaton (Grimm et al. 2006, appendix 
        A p136-147). Note that in the case of cellular automaton, some of the 
        design concepts of the ODD protocol do not apply.</p>

<h4>State variables and scales</h4>
<p>The model is based on biological and ecological rules derived from field and laboratory experimental data for the three PTM species (Dangles & Carpio 2008; Dangles et al. 2008; Roux and Baumg&auml;rtner 1997). The Simiatug valley, used as a pilot region to build the model, is located in the province of Bolívar, in the central highlands of Ecuador. We focused on a study area of 20 x 20 km represented by a grid of 1,600 cells with a cell size of 0.25 km&sup2;. Each cell <i>i</i> is characterized by its quality of habitat Q<sub>i</sub> i.e. the quantity of food resources available for the moth larvae. Q<sub>i</sub> is a boolean variable which depends on the land use (ie. crops or other uses such as woods or highlands). Each cell is also characterized by a range of temperature per cell <i>i</i> and month <i>j</i> T<sub>i,j</sub> (in ºC), an average amount of precipitation per cell <i>i</i> and month <i>j</i> L<sub>i,j</sub> (in mm), and a mean elevation per cell <i>i</i> E<sub>i</sub> (in m.a.s.l.). </p>
<p><b>Table 1.</b> State and higher-level variables of SimPolilla</p>
