<html>
<head>
<meta name="KeyWords" content="Cormas, Cirad, gestion des ressources naturelles, natural resource management, INRM, environnement, environment, simulation, modelisation, modelling, SMA, MAS, multi-agent, smalltalk, visualworks, logiciel, software">
<title> Cormas Description</title>
<meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=iso-8859-1">

<!--#include virtual="/include/script.inc" -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/cormas.css" -->
</head>
<body  marginwidth="0" marginheight="0" topmargin="0" leftmargin="0" border="0" onLoad="MM_preloadImages(/images/en/accueil_r.gif,/images/en/demarche_r.gif,/images/en/logiciel_r.gif,/images/en/applications_r.gif,/images/en/biblio_r.gif,/images/en/formation_r.gif,/images/en/reseaux_r.gif)" bgcolor="#FFFFFF">
<!--#include virtual="haut.inc" -->
<table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" width="100%">
<tr>
    <!--#include virtual="sommair.inc" --> 
    <td width="*" valign="top" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"> 
      <p>&nbsp;</p>
      <h1>Cormas description</h1>
      <p>&nbsp;</p>
      <p>There exist more and more programming environments dedicated to the creation 
        of multi-agent systems. Some of them are oriented towards communication 
        between distributed systems, as some others are more oriented towards 
        the building of simulation models (see <a href="../reseaux/liens.htm">links 
        page</a>).</p>
      <p>The CORMAS programming environment belongs to this second category, with 
        a specificity in the domain of natural-resources management. It provides 
        a framework for developing simulation models of coordination modes between 
        individuals and groups who jointly exploit the resources. This framework 
        is structured in the following three modules (see figure).</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The first module allows the definition of the entities of the system 
          to be modeled, which are called informatics agents, and their interactions. 
          These interactions are expressed by direct communication procedures 
          (sending of messages) and/or by the fact of sharing the same spatial 
          support.</li>
        <li>The second module deals with the control of the overall dynamics (ordering 
          of different events during a time-step of the model).</li>
        <li>The third module allows the definition of an observation of the simulation 
          depending on viewpoints. This feature allows the integration, within 
          the modelling process, of representation modes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>&nbsp;</p>
      <p align="center"> <img src="/images/en/cormas.gif"></p>
      <p align="center" class="texte">Cormas interface</p>
      <p>Cormas facilitates the work of constructing a model by offering predefined 
        elements within these three modules. Among these items are the Cormas 
        entities, which are SmallTalk generic classes from which, by specialization 
        and refining, the user can create entities specific to the needs of his 
        application.</p>
      <p>Cormas is a simulation platform based on the VisualWorks programming 
        environment which allows the development of applications in SmallTalk.</p>
      <p>Cormas can be <a href="outchar.htm">downloaded</a>. <a href="outguid.htm">Users 
        guides</a> are also available. The main <a href="uml-kernel.htm">UML class 
        diagrams</a> of the Cormas kernel are available.</p>
      <!--#include virtual="/include/copyright_en.inc" --> </td>
    <td width="15" valign="top" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"> 
    <div align="right" class="texte"></div>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>
</body>
</html>
<html>
<head>
<meta name="KeyWords" content="Cormas, Cirad, gestion des ressources naturelles, natural resource management, INRM, environnement, environment, simulation, modelisation, modelling, SMA, MAS, multi-agent, smalltalk, visualworks, logiciel, software">
<title> Cormas: first models</title>
<meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=iso-8859-1">

<!--#include virtual="/include/script.inc" -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/cormas.css" -->
</head>
<body  marginwidth="0" marginheight="0" topmargin="0" leftmargin="0" border="0" onLoad="MM_preloadImages(/images/en/accueil_r.gif,/images/en/demarche_r.gif,/images/en/logiciel_r.gif,/images/en/applications_r.gif,/images/en/biblio_r.gif,/images/en/formation_r.gif,/images/en/reseaux_r.gif)" bgcolor="#FFFFFF">
<!--#include virtual="haut.inc" -->
<table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" width="100%">
<tr>
    <!--#include virtual="sommair.inc" --> 
    <td width="*" valign="top" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"> 
 <p>&nbsp;
      <h1>Download the first models</h1>
      <p>&nbsp;</p>
          
      <p>In order to test Cormas, you can download the following models:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>four didactic models 
          <ul>
            <li>An example of cellular automata [<a href="/en/applica/fireautomata.htm">FireAutomata</a>], 
            </li>
            <li>An example of situated agents [<a href="/en/applica/pursuit.htm">Pursuit</a>], 
            </li>
            <li>An example of communicating agents [<a href="/en/applica/plotsrental.htm">PlotsRental</a>]</li>
            <li>An example of aggregative spatial entities [<a href="/en/applica/tse.htm">TSE</a>]</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>two theoretical models 
          <ul>
            <li>The famous Axtell and Epstein model [<a href="/en/applica/sugarScape.htm">SugarScape</a>]</li>
            <li>Emergence of Resource-Sharing Conventions [<a href="/en/applica/dricol.htm">Dricol</a>]</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>one applied model 
          <ul>
            <li>Hunting of wild meat in Cameroon [<a href="/en/applica/djemiong.htm">Djemiong</a>]</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Follow <a href="import.htm">this link</a> to install those models. </p>
      <p>In addition, a library of downloadable models is available from the <a href="/en/applica/applica.htm">applications</a> 
        pages. Some of them appear as downloadable zip archives.</p>
      <p>You are invited to subscribe to <a href="/en/reseaux/forum.htm">Cormas 
        list</a> to keep yourself informed and to communicate with other Cormas 
        users.</p>
      <p></p>
    <!--#include virtual="/include/copyright_en.inc" -->
	</td>
    <td width="15" valign="top" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"> 
    <div align="right" class="texte"></div>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>
</body>
</html>
<html>

<head>

<meta name="KeyWords" content="Cormas, Cirad, gestion des ressources naturelles, natural resource management, INRM, environnement, environment, simulation, modelisation, modelling, SMA, MAS, multi-agent, smalltalk, visualworks, logiciel, software">

<title> Cormas Users Guides</title>

<meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=iso-8859-1">



<!--#include virtual="/include/script.inc" -->

<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/cormas.css" -->

</head>

<body  marginwidth="0" marginheight="0" topmargin="0" leftmargin="0" border="0" onLoad="MM_preloadImages(/images/en/accueil_r.gif,/images/en/demarche_r.gif,/images/en/logiciel_r.gif,/images/en/applications_r.gif,/images/en/biblio_r.gif,/images/en/formation_r.gif,/images/en/reseaux_r.gif)" bgcolor="#FFFFFF">

<!--#include virtual="haut.inc" -->

<table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0" width="100%">

<tr>

    <!--#include virtual="sommair.inc" --> 

    <td width="*" valign="top" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"> 

 <p>

      <h1>Cormas Users Guides</h1>

      <h2>&nbsp;</h2>

      <h2>Tutorials </h2>

      <p><img src="../../images/new1.gif" width="40" height="20"> Two tutorials 

        are available.With the first one (<a href="../../pdf/CormasTutorial1.pdf">CormasTutorial1.pdf</a>), 

        the user can manipulate an existing example (Game of Life: <a href="../../logiciel/Conway.zip">Conway.zip</a>) 

        and then prepare its own model from scratch. A cellular automata simulates 

        the diffusion of a fire. A set of firemen agents try to extinguish the 

        fire. They will improve their efficiency by exchanging messages, and by 

        considering fire at different scales. </p>

      <p>With the second tutorial (<a href="../../pdf/CormasTutorial2.pdf">CormasTutorial2.pdf</a>), 

        the user will also build a model from scratch. The model is inspired from 

        a paper by Pepper and Smuts, &quot;Evolution of Cooperation in an Ecological 

        Context&quot; (download the <a href="http://www.santafe.edu/%7Ejpepper/papers/%20Pepper&Smuts2000.pdf">PDF</a> 

        at Santa Fe Institut). The survival of two populations of agents depends 

        on the spatial configuration. The user will also learn how to conduct 

        a sensitivity analysis and the different ways to save the results of a 

        simulation (download the model: <a href="../../logiciel/MyFirstCormasModel.zip">MyFirstCormasModel.zip</a>).</p>

      <h2>Users guide</h2>

      <p>A Users guide will be available soon, describing architecture of Cormas 
        and explaining the main classes and methods. Nevertheless, the main <a href="uml-kernel.htm">UML 
        class diagrams</a> of the Cormas kernel are available. About the links 
        between Cormas and some commercial GIS softwares (MapInfo, Idrisi, ArcView), 
        you may have a look to this <a href="/pdf/GIS_Cormas.pdf">Cormas/GIS guide</a>.</p>

      <h2>Smalltalk free books</h2>

      <p>Some Smalltalk books are freely downloadable. You can find them on the 

        St&eacute;phane Ducasses web site : <a href="http://www.iam.unibe.ch/~ducasse/FreeBooks.html" target="_blank">http://www.iam.unibe.ch/~ducasse/FreeBooks.html</a></p>

      <p>&nbsp;</p>

